opose a longer-term solution — potentially as the next major project instead of the previously discussed upgrade. Susan attempted a temporary workaround via customer-specific pricing, which is not yet displaying correctly (possibly due to indexing), and will continue looking for a temporary fix while the root cause is addressed. Separately, the reorder action feature was confirmed working on staging by the client (it remembers previous order notes and directs to the product page) and was approved for deployment to production. Nour also noted approximately 20+ remaining budget hours and will check with JP on how to handle them.','Meeting Summary and Next Steps – Unknown Client','<p>Hi Susan,</p><p>Thank you for taking the time to meet with us on Friday, July 31, 2026. Here\'s a summary of our discussion and agreed next steps:</p><h3>Key Points Discussed</h3><ul><li><strong>Urgent pricing issue:</strong> A Calgary Walmart customer was shown an incorrect price ($75 instead of $90) on a product (PK code 752). Group pricing is currently not overriding catalog price rules, affecting all Walmart locations across Canada that share the same catalog pricing.</li><li><strong>Pricing hierarchy confirmed:</strong> The expected priority order is (1) price per customer, (2) group price, (3) catalog price rule.</li><li><strong>Temporary workaround:</strong> A customer-specific pricing entry was attempted as a stopgap but is not yet displaying correctly (possibly an indexing issue). This workaround, even if successful, does not replace the need for a full fix of the pricing hierarchy.</li><li><strong>Reorder action feature:</strong> Confirmed working on staging — it retains notes from the previous order and directs users to the product page. Approved for deployment to production.</li><li><strong>Remaining budget hours:</strong> Approximately 20+ hours remain; handling of these will be confirmed.</li></ul><h3>Decisions Made</h3><ul><li>The pricing issue will be raised as a critical bug and prioritized for immediate investigation.</li><li>JP will lead a deeper investigation into the pricing structure to propose a longer-term solution; this may be prioritized as the next project over the previously discussed upgrade.</li><li>The reorder action feature is approved and will be deployed to production as soon as possible.</li></ul><h3>Action Items</h3><table border=\"1\" cellpadding=\"6\" cellspacing=\"0\"><tr><th>Action Item</th><th>Owner</th><th>Due Date</th></tr><tr><td>Raise a critical bug and investigate the group pricing / catalog pricing override issue (starting on staging)</td><td>Nour (Technopath)</td><td>ASAP</td></tr><tr><td>Investigate the pricing structure and propose a longer-term pricing hierarchy solution</td><td>JP (Technopath)</td><td>TBD</td></tr><tr><td>Deploy the reorder action feature to production</td><td>Nour (Technopath)</td><td>ASAP</td></tr><tr><td>Continue exploring a temporary workaround to display correct pricing (incl. customer-specific pricing)</td><td>Susan (Create Pharmacy)</td><td>ASAP</td></tr><tr><td>Confirm handling of remaining budget hours</td><td>Nour (Technopath)</td><td>TBD</td></tr></table><h3>Open Questions</h3><ul><li>Whether the pricing issue is reproducible on staging (to be verified after the meeting).</li><li>Why customer-specific pricing is not displaying correctly (possible indexing issue).</li><li>How the remaining budget hours (~20+) will be allocated or carried forward.</li><li>Prioritization of the pricing hierarchy project versus the platform upgrade discussion.</li></ul><h3>Next Steps</h3><p>Our team will begin investigating the pricing issue immediately and will follow up with findings and a proposed fix. We will also deploy the reorder action feature to production shortly.</p><p>Please don\'t hesitate to reach out if anything needs clarification.</p><p>Best,</p>','Meeting Summary and Next Steps – Unknown Client','<p>Hi Susan,</p><p>Thank you for taking the time to meet with us on Friday, Jul