INSERT INTO `recommendations` VALUES (1,14,'1. **GA4/Adobe Commerce Conversion Rate**: From **73.33% to 4.25%**, based on order/user data spanning the investigation period.\n2. **GA4 vs Adobe Commerce**: On **July 14**, GA4 recorded **146 sessions**, but Adobe Commerce showed only **4 orders**, contrasting sharply with earlier similar sessions resulting in more conversions.\n3. **Clarity Correlation**: Friction score peaked at **34.3** on **July 14**, with a corresponding rise in script errors (205), on a day where conversions were notably low.','**What Happened**\nThe conversion rate decreased significantly by **94.2%**, dropping from **73.33%** with **44 orders** and **60 users** to **4.25%** with **74 orders** and **1,740 users** over the previous month compared to the anomaly period.\n\n| Metric | Prior Period | Anomaly Period | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Orders | 44 | 74 | Increase |\n| Users | 60 | 1,740 | Significant Increase |\n| Conversion Rate | 73.33% | 4.25% | Decrease (-94.2%) |\n\n**Cross-Platform Evidence**\n- **GA4 vs Adobe Commerce**: Discrepancies in session recording occurred; GA4 reported sessions with little overlap to the Adobe Commerce order data.\n- Traffic source analysis from GA4 shows a high number of sessions from direct (consistently about **30%** - **50%**), but low transactions particularly in the latter period.\n- **July 14** stands out with **146** GA4 sessions but only **4 orders** recorded in Adobe Commerce, marking a low conversion despite high engagement.\n\n**Behavioral Analysis**\n- Clarity data on **July 14** shows a spike in **rage clicks** (6), **dead clicks** (118), and **quick backs** (146).\n- Throughout the anomaly period, engagement issues were evident, with frequent spikes in dead clicks and script errors coinciding with decreased conversion rates.\n> There was a significant **friction score increase** from typical mid-teens to **34.3** on July 14, linking directly to user frustrations emanating from technical issues as evidenced by elevated script errors.\n\n**Root Cause Assessment**\n- Likely root causes include technical errors evidenced by increased script errors on specific days, impacting user experience and conversion.\n- Disparities in session tracking between GA4 and Adobe Commerce highlight data integrity issues, which may affect reported conversion rates.','The confirmed root cause of the conversion rate dip is heavily linked to technical issues as evidenced by spikes in script errors and elevated user friction scores during peak traffic days, specifically on July 14. These technical challenges likely frustrated users, leading to abandoned shopping activities despite high engagement on the platform. The impact is evident in the drastic decline in the conversion rate from **73.33% to 4.25%**, and adjustment is necessary to mitigate further revenue loss and customer attrition. Immediate actions must involve debugging and addressing reported script errors across critical site usage points, ensuring data consistency between GA4 and Adobe Commerce for better conversion tracking, and rectifying high friction interaction points as observed from Clarity data to enhance user engagement and overall site reliability.','openai/gpt-4o','2026-07-15 02:00:46','2026-07-15 02:00:46','internal',0),(2,15,'1. **Adobe Commerce Conversion Rate**: 0% daily during anomaly period — sessions reported with 0 conversion indicating script failure.\n2. **GA4 vs Adobe Commerce**: On July 3, GA4 showed 2,354 sessions but Adobe Commerce recorded only 16 orders (0.68% conversion), compared to July 1 sessions/orders — a clear reporting mismatch.\n3. **Clarity Correlation**: Script errors surged to 6,534 on July 13, corresponding with complete drop in revenue/logged conversions, supporting technical errors as a cause.\n4. **Klaviyo Influence**: Email open rates consistently above 40%, suggesting normal engagement levels, thus email not a direct cause of poor conversion.','**What Happened**\n\nThe conversion rate suffered a **97.4% decrease** from **33.89%** in the prior period to **0.90%** in the anomaly period. Orders decreased from **446** to **636** despite users increasing from **1,316** to **70,967**.\n\n| Metric | Prior Period | Anomaly Period | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Conversion Rate | 33.89% | 0.90% | -97.4% |\n| Orders | 446 | 636 | +42.6% |\n| Active Users | 1,316 | 70,967 | +5292.3% |\n| Revenue | N/A | N/A | N/A |\n\n\n**Cross-Platform Evidence**\n\n- **GA4 Data**: Sessions were stable, ranging from **2,354** to **2,946** daily, yet orders on Adobe Commerce remained consistent at **12-30 orders** per day despite massive user traffic.\n- **July 1 - 5**: Sessions on GA4 each day were over **2,000**, but orders and revenues on Adobe Commerce faced discrepancies due to **0% conversion rate** reported.\n- **Traffic Channel Analysis**: Email, organic, and paid channels showed similar session counts as before, but no corresponding increase in conversions was detected in Adobe Commerce.\n\n| Date | GA4 Sessions | Adobe Orders | Gap |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| July 1 | 2,088 | 24 | Orders/Users mismatch |\n| July 3 | 2,354 | 16 | Orders/Users mismatch |\n| July 14 | 35 | 0 | Complete anomaly |\n\n\n**Behavioral Analysis**\n\n- Clarity showed an anomalous increase in **script errors** beginning July 1, peaking at **6,534** on July 13 without any deployment logs indicating changes.\n- Identified friction with **quick backs** consistently above **700**, indicating site difficulty for users.\n> Significant script errors and dead clicks may have rendered checkout processes non-functional.\n\n\n**Root Cause Assessment**\n\nThe primary root cause appears to be a critical client-side issue, possibly a JavaScript error, leading to the erroneous report of **0% conversion rates** by Adobe Commerce despite active sessions and stable behavioral metrics from GA4. Clarity data demonstrates high frustration signals coupled with technical errors impacting checkout processes repeatedly on multiple days.','The confirmed root cause of the drastic conversion rate drop across platforms was primarily a failure in Adobe Commerce\'s tracking and reporting systems, suggested by a surge in script errors and unusual zero conversion rates while GA4 was recording standard session levels. This technical error likely led to a misrepresentation of the actual conversion and user interaction data, resulting in inaccurately reported decreases and revenue recognition. Due to these reporting errors, direct quantification of revenue impact is challenging, though user engagement metrics suggest the potential for significant transactional loss given typical conversion rates. Immediate action should involve resolving these client-side scripting issues within Adobe Commerce and cross-verifying with live data versus recorded metrics to ensure accurate figures and restore operational tracking integrity.','openai/gpt-4o','2026-07-15 02:01:25','2026-07-15 02:01:25','internal',0),(3,16,'1. **GA4 Traffic**: Direct traffic on July 1 achieved **601 sessions** with only **6 transactions** with revenue of **$2,488.63** — reveals low direct conversion.\n2. **Clarity Correlation**: Friction score increased from **26.2** on July 1 to **31.2** on July 2; script errors surged on July 13 (**6,534 errors** paralleled revenue drops).\n3. **Adobe Commerce**: Revenue imbalance on July 12 where **14 orders** with **$2,410.84** opposed the session data.\n4. **GA4 vs Adobe Commerce (July 6)**: **2,946 sessions** in GA4 but **30 orders** in Adobe Commerce indicating potential session tracking issues.\n\n','**What Happened**\nThe Cart → Checkout abandonment rate increased from **33.1%** to **38.3%** (a **15.5%** increase) over the last month, resulting in a drop from **154** checkouts to **103**.\n\n| Metric | Prior Period | Anomaly Period | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Checkout Abandonment Rate | 33.1% | 38.3% | **15.5%** up |\n| Checkouts | 154 | 