f **26.5**.\n- Despite steady engagement scores, there were significant **quick backs** (e.g., **1,631** on July 14), signaling potential UX issues causing discontent.\n\n**Root Cause Assessment**\n> The decline in traffic is primarily attributed to technical issues indicated by high scripts/error clicks and friction scores without a complete drop in customer intent as shown by stable order rates in Adobe Commerce.\n- The absence of New Relic error logs confirms that the issue lies more likely in front-end website errors affecting user path consistency.\n- Email and SMS conversions didn\'t show significant deviations, ruling out marketing communications effectiveness as a primary cause.','The principal root cause of the reduced sessions appears to be a technical issue, particularly high script errors and quick back signals in the Clarity data. These issues are disrupting the user experience, reflected by fruitless session increases without numeric conversion drops on Adobe Commerce metrics.\nQuantitatively, this issue translates to a session loss over **9,750** sessions compared to the prior month period, whereas conversion rates have remained stable in periods of highest error logs (e.g., July 14-18).\nImmediate action should involve resolving front-end script issues and optimizing website loading and interaction reliability, as proactive steps will potentially restore lost sessions by improving user engagement, thereby bridging the session-revenue discrepancy currently evident.','openai/gpt-4o','2026-07-28 02:01:28','2026-07-28 02:01:28','internal',0),(20,35,'1. **Clarity Dead Clicks**: Increased from **13.5** to **17** by August 1.\n2. **GA4 vs Adobe Commerce**: On July 27, GA4 showed **91 sessions**, but Adobe Commerce recorded **4 orders**, indicating a **4.4% conversion**.\n3. **Clarity Correlation**: Friction score on July 30 increased from **14.7** to **22** with dead clicks at **18**; same day had high engagement time **202s**.\n4. **New Relic Page Load Time**: Increase to **6.58s** on July 27 indicating potential backend issues, affecting user interaction.','**What Happened**\nThe dead click rate increased by **26.2%** from **13.5** to **17** clicks per day, detected on August 1. This may indicate confusion due to non-interactive elements appearing clickable.\n\n| Metric | Prior Period | Anomaly Period | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Dead Clicks | 13.5 | 17 | **26.2% Increase** |\n\n**Cross-Platform Evidence**\nComparing GA4 sessions with Adobe Commerce orders between July 18 and August 1:\n\n- **GA4 Traffic**: Consistent with prior periods, but conversion remained **0** for most days.\n- **Adobe Commerce**: Orders fluctuated with highest on July 27 (**4 orders**) despite sessions being zero, matches with GA4 which shows **91 sessions**.\n- Cross-network contributed significant traffic but revenue attributed to paid search was highest on July 29, with **$286.29**.\n\n| Date | GA4 Sessions | Adobe Orders | Conversion Rate |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| July 20 | 72 | 3 | 4.17% |\n| July 27 | 91 | 4 | 4.40% |\n| July 29 | 85 | 3 | 3.53% |\n\n**Behavioral Analysis**\nClarity data highlights significant fluctuations in dead clicks:\n\n- July 21: Dead clicks peaked at **15**, friction score high **17.3**.\n- July 31 - August 1: Friction score spikes from **14.7** to **22** with corresponding increase in dead clicks from **18** to **7**.\n\n> July 30: High engagement time **202** seconds paired with **18** dead clicks implies possible confusion or misaligned CTAs.\n\n**Root Cause Assessment**\nThe likely root cause is the increase in non-intuitive interactive elements causing user frustration, as evidenced by dead click metrics rising and corresponding engagement without conversion. A notable correlation between dead clicks, GA4 funnel drop-offs, and non-optimal New Relic page load times (e.g., **6.58**s on July 27) supports this conclusion.','The analysis confirms that the root cause of the increased dead clicks is linked to changes in page layout or CTA design leading to user confusion. Key behavioral signals, particularly the rise in dead clicks and high engagement times without corresponding conversion improvements, underline usability issues. This has resulted in some fluctuation in order patterns and a potential loss in optimal user pathways.\nThe impact includes an overall friction increase leading to users disengaging before completing transactions, as seen on specific high traffic days (e.g., July 30 with **290** engagements). Immediate design adjustment is recommended to redefine interactive elements clearly to users and to streamline the path to conversion, thereby reducing dead clicks and optimizing the buyer journey.','openai/gpt-4o','2026-08-01 02:00:37','2026-08-01 02:00:37','internal',0),(21,36,'1. **GA4 Active Users**: Dropped from **8,034** to **1,741** — major factor in conversion rate spike\n2. **GA4 vs Adobe Commerce**: On July 20, **72** sessions yielded only **1** GA4 order, while Adobe recorded **3 orders**, showing marked differences in attribution\n3. **Clarity Correlation**: Friction scores peaked at **57.3** with **398** script errors on July 25 correlating with subsequent conversion correction\n4. **New Relic Page Load Times**: Varied significantly, peaking at **7.41s** on July 25 possibly affecting user experience negatively leading to high friction\n5. **July 30 Spike**: **290** traffic from Clarity with heightened engagement indicated returning users correcting prior technical detraction','**What Happened**\nA significant anomaly was observed with the conversion rate (orders ÷ users) increasing by **478.7%** month-over-month due to a substantial decrease in GA4 active users by **78.3%** and a smaller relative increase in Adobe Commerce orders by **25.4%**.\n\n| Metric | Prior Period | Anomaly Period | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Conversion Rate | **0.78%** | **4.54%** | **478.7%** |\n| Orders | **63** | **79** | **25.4%** |\n| Active Users | **8,034** | **1,741** | **-78.3%** |\n\n**Cross-Platform Evidence**\n- GA4 shows a decline in sessions from **8,034** to **1,741** while Adobe Commerce orders increased from **63** to **79** over the same period.\n\n- On July 20, GA4 recorded **72** sessions with revenue of **$74.24** generated from **1** order, whereas Adobe Commerce registered **3** orders with **Revenue** **$1,092.75** , inciting attention to possible session attribution or order count issues.\n\n**Behavioral Analysis**\n- Clarity data indicates on July 30, a substantial increase in engagement traffic to **290** from average figures, paired with script errors rising to **84** and **quick backs** at **149**, potentially indicating a content or technical glitch coinciding with order behavior anomalies.\n\n- High script errors and dead clicks on July 25 with **398** script errors, the highest friction score **57.3**, preceding a spike in Adobe orders **3** on July 29 suggests technical issues being resolved.\n\n> **High friction scores on July 25 coincided with a later upsurge in purchases suggesting remediation action or technical fix.**\n\n**Root Cause Assessment**\n- Likely root cause may involve misattribution of sessions vs. orders across platforms or data capture changes on site delivery affecting user tracking. Clarity data and script errors point towards technical issues being addressed towards the anomaly period end.','The pronounced increase in the conversion rate is largely attributed to a significant decrease in reported user sessions per GA4 without a proportionate decline in order numbers from Adobe Commerce. This suggests discrepancies in data tracking and session attribution potentially due to platform integration issues or data capture concerns that may have been partially mitigated by the end of July.\n\nClarity data revealed high script errors and engagement frictions during July 25, indicating technical challenges potentially residing in site infrastructure or transaction processes affecting user sentiment and conversion behavior. The subsequent recovery of engagement and purchases suggests these issues began to resolve, reaffirming the hypothesis of technical constraints being addressed just before the anomaly notably emerged.\n\nImmediate action should focus on verifying cross-platform data accuracy to eliminate false session impressions and ensuring robust monitoring of site technical performance to prevent misalignment in user behavior capture and conversion recording.','openai/gpt-4o','2026-08-01 02:00:47','2026-08-01 02:00:47','internal',0),(22,37,'1. **Clarity Dead Clicks**: 42.0% increase on **August 2, 2026** - Clearly reflects broken interactive elements\n2. **GA4 vs Adobe Commerce**: On **July 29, 2026**, GA4 had 85 sessions; Adobe Commerce had 4 orders (4.71% conversion) vs. high traffic day **112 sessions** and zero orders on **July 31**\n3. **Clarity Correlation**: Friction score spike from **17.3 to 57.3** on **July 25** with a direct impact on orders\n4. **New Relic Performance**: Consistent TTFB > 1s from **July 25-26**, impacting load times and user behavior\n5. **GA4 Traffic Drop**: Paid and direct sessions together drop from **65 on July 30** to 14 on **August 1**, contributing to decreased order totals','**What Happened**\nThere was a **42.0% increase in dead clicks** (from 12 to 17) which occurred simultaneously with a **10.1% drop in revenue**. This anomaly was detected on **August 2, 2026**.\n\n| Metric | Prior Period | Anomaly Period | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Revenue | $1,675 | $1,506 | **-10.1%** |\n| Dead Clicks | 12 | 17 | **42.0%** |\n| Conversion Rate | 1.56% | 1.11% | **-29.1%** |\n| Sessions | 808 | 190 | **-76.5%** |\n\n**Cross-Platform Evidence**\n- **GA4** data from July 29-30 shows **179 sessions** with a conversion rate of **3.56%**, while Adobe Commerce reported **8 orders** totaling _$994.45_.\n- **July 31-August 1:** Sessions dropped from 112 to 56, while orders fell to only 3, a sharp decline in engagement.\n- **Paid Traffic Contribution**: On July 29, paid search drove **26 sessions** with $286.29 revenue, but on August 1, paid traffic contributed to only 6 sessions and no revenue.\n\n**Behavioral Analysis**\n- **Clarity data** shows significant spikes in dead clicks around **July 21** (15 dead clicks) and **July 30-31** (18 dead clicks), aligning with the revenue drops on these days.\n- **Script Errors**: Peaked on July 25 with **398 errors**, leading to a friction score of **57.3**.\n- > Critical friction on **July 25**: A massive surge in quick backs (**57 instances**) and script errors led to user dissatisfaction.\n\n**Root Cause Assessment**\n- Likely root is malfunctioning buttons or images as evident from 42.0% more dead clicks.\n- Desynchronized traffic from performance issues and high friction derived from script errors, especially on **July 25**.\n- **Performance metrics** from New Relic show a server response time increase (> **1 sec** TTFB) correlating with high friction and behavior disruption.','The investigation reveals that the root cause of revenue decline is linked to broken interactive elements like buttons or images, evidenced by a **42.0% surge in dead clicks** coinciding with user friction and revenue decline. Issues on **July 25, 2026**, marked the crucial turning point with severe script errors and quick backs indicating dissatisfied user interaction.\n\nThe financial impact has been notable with an overall **$169 revenue drop** in the period analyzed and a significant **29.1% drop in conversion rate**. The drop in user sessions was a staggering **76.5%**, severely limiting customer acquisition and engagement.\n\nImmediate action should include auditing and fixing the broken interactive components to reduce dead clicks and address the high script error rate, especially focusing on the impactful date ranges of **July 25 onwards**. This will recover lost revenue opportunities and improve user experience crucial for retaining traffic and maximizing sales.','openai/gpt-4o','2026-08-02 02:01:11','2026-08-02 02:01:11','internal',0),(23,38,'1. **GA4 Metric**: Conversion rate decreased to **1.08%** over 30 days.\n2. **GA4 vs Adobe Commerce**: On **July 21**, GA4 had **357 sessions** with **2 orders** (conversion rate of **2.47%**), whereas Adobe Commerce showed fewer orders.\n3. **Email Campaigns**: Klaviyo showed a revenue drop from **$7,020.78** on **July 21** to $0 on **August 3**, indicating reduced effectiveness over time.','**What Happened**\nThe conversion rate decreased from **1.26%** to **1.08%**, a **14.4%** change over the past 30 days. This anomaly was detected on **August 4, 2026**.\n\n| Metric | Prior Period | Anomaly Period | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Conversion Rate | **1.26%** | **1.08%** | **-14.4%** |\n\n**Cross-Platform Evidence**\nCross-referencing data from GA4 and Adobe Commerce highlights discrepancies in session and order trends.\n\n| Date | GA4 Sessions | Adobe Orders | Conversion Rate |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 2026-07-21 | **357** | **2** | **2.47%** |\n| 2026-07-24 | **167** | **2** | **0.94%** |\n| 2026-07-27 | **318** | **4** | **4.4%** |\n\n- GA4 shows a high session count but fluctuating conversion rates.\n- Adobe Commerce recorded several days with zero orders despite substantial GA4 sessions (e.g., July 21 and July 30).\n- Most conversions seem to stem from direct traffic.\n\n**Behavioral Analysis**\nNo Clarity data was available to directly correlate behavioral signals.\n\n**Root Cause Assessment**\nThe most likely root cause is a discrepancy between session tracking in GA4 and order recording in Adobe Commerce. Fluctuations in direct traffic, coupled with consistent high bounce rates, suggest conversion friction.\n\n> Adobe Commerce recordings had zero orders on multiple days despite decent traffic, indicating potential integration or data alignment issues.','The primary root cause of the conversion rate decline seems to be integration or data alignment issues between GA4 sessions and Adobe Commerce order records, resulting in unreliable conversion tracking. There was a noticeable drop in conversions even when sessions remained stable, particularly around direct traffic sources. The impact of the anomaly has likely led to significant revenue discrepancies, specifically a marked reduction in effective conversion attribution. This warrants a priority fix in ensuring accurate data synchronization between analytics platforms and ecommerce systems, and possibly re-evaluating direct traffic conversion funnels to mitigate friction and restore reliability in data reports.','openai/gpt-4o','2026-08-04 02:00:58','2026-08-04 02:00:58','internal',0),(24,40,'1. **GA4 Users**: Increased from **3,039** to **4,993** (July 21 - Aug 3)\n2. **GA4 vs Adobe Commerce**: On July 25, **259 sessions** but only **1 order** (0.39%), vs July 21, **357 sessions** / **2 orders** (0.56%)\n3. **Klaviyo Revenue**: Dropped from **$7,839.60** across campaigns on July 21 to **$0** on Aug 3\n4. **Adobe Commerce Orders**: **45** to **29** (June 22 - July 21 compared to July 22 - Aug 3)\n5. **Adobe Commerce Avg Order Value**: Significant spikes, e.g., July 28 ($4,013.51) affecting perception of order decline margin\n','**What Happened**\nThe unified conversion rate declined by **60.8%**, from **1.48%** to **0.58%**, comparing the last 30 days to the prior 30-day period. Orders dropped from **45** to **29**, while active users increased from **3,039** to **4,993**.\n\n| Metric | Prior Period | Anomaly Period | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Orders | 45 | 29 | **-35.6%** |\n| Active Users | 3,039 | 4,993 | **+64.3%** |\n| Conversion Rate | 1.48% | 0.58% | **-60.8%** |\n\n**Cross-Platform Evidence**\nComparison between GA4 and Adobe Commerce highlights discrepancies:\n- GA4 showed consistent sessions but uneven order registrations on Adobe Commerce.\n- On July 25, GA4 recorded **259 sessions** but Adobe Commerce logged only **1 order**, a drop to **0.39% conversion**, compared to **2 orders** on July 21 with **357 sessions**.\n\n| Date | GA4 Sessions | Adobe Orders | Conversion |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| July 21 | 357 | 2 | **0.56%** |\n| July 25 | 259 | 1 | **0.39%** |\n| Aug 3 | 201 | 4 | **1.99%** |\n\n**Behavioral Analysis**\nClarity data absence limits contextual understanding of user behavior issues like rage or dead clicks. Without these insights, focusing on available sales and session metrics remains crucial.\n\n**Root Cause Assessment**\nThe primary issue appears to be a sharp increase in user sessions not translating to sales:\n- Uncoordinated spikes in users without proportional order rises\n- Reduced email marketing efficacy with Klaviyo\'s declined order impact\n\n> The significant user increase lacking transaction conversion indicates possible issues beyond user acquisition, potentially UX or product page-related.','The investigation isolated the root cause of conversion decline to be a mismatch between user session volume and sales performance on Adobe Commerce. Despite increasing traffic and user activity by **64.3%**, conversion fell by **60.8%**, indicating issues either in the conversion funnel or in gaining meaningful engagement from the new users.\n\nThis misalignment resulted in missed sales opportunities, with order counts decreasing by **35.6%** and an apparent drop in email effectiveness, evidenced by Klaviyo\'s campaign revenue falling to zero.\n\nTo rectify this, focus must be placed on enhancing user experience across the site to ensure that user interest converts into sales. This includes potential UX/UI improvements, checkout flow optimization, and better alignment of marketing strategies with traffic acquisition methods to match user volume to order potential.','openai/gpt-4o','2026-08-04 02:01:27','2026-08-04 02:01:27','internal',0),(25,41,'1. **GA4 Metric**: Session retention on July 21 showed **357 sessions**, 0 Adobe orders — high disconnect\n2. **Adobe Commerce Revenue**: **$4013.51 on July 28** with only 1 session implies data mismatch\n3. **Klaviyo Emails**: **0 revenue from Abandoned Cart Flow** on August 3, a drop from **$7020.78** on July 21\n4. **Overall Conversion**: Stagnated at **0.4%-0.5%** on critical days during anomaly period','**What Happened**\nThe Cart → Checkout abandonment rate increased from **9.4% to 11.4%**, a **20.5% increase**, in the past 30 days compared to the previous period.\n\n| Metric | Prior Period | Anomaly Period | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Cart → Checkout | 191 to 173 | 229 to 203 | 20.5% increase |\n\n**Cross-Platform Evidence**\n- **GA4** shows a decrease in conversion rates despite similar traffic volume.\n- **Adobe Commerce** recorded inconsistent order counts with corresponding GA4 sessions.\n\n| Date | GA4 Sessions | Adobe Orders | Conversion Rate |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| July 21 | 357 | 0 | Discrepancy |\n| July 27 | 318 | 4 | 1.26% |\n| August 1 | 129 | 1 | 0.78% |\n\n- High-value order anomalies on **July 28** and **August 3** did not correlate with sessions.\n- **Unassigned** and **direct traffic** consistently had lower conversions across both platforms.\n\n**Behavioral Analysis**\nBehavioral data from Clarity was unavailable, restricting analysis of user behavior issues or engagement friction.\n\n**Root Cause Assessment**\n- Discrepancies between GA4 sessions and Adobe Commerce orders suggest tracking or data integration issues.\n- Email marketing engagement declined sharply, evidenced by a reduction in flows and conversions on crucial dates, impacting the funnel negatively.\n\n> The sharp decrease in Klaviyo engagement (from 8 conversions on July 21 to 0 on August 3) aligns with increased abandonment rates, indicating potential email campaign influence.','The evidence points to a likely root cause of data integration issues between GA4 and Adobe Commerce, impacting the accuracy of conversion metrics and hindering optimization efforts. Additionally, the pronounced decline in Klaviyo-attributed revenues indicates that email campaigns did not retain customer engagement effectively. This misalignment led to increased checkout abandonment rates and possible revenue loss, notably impacting ecommerce performance.\n\nTo resolve the issue, immediate reconciling of data integration between GA4 and Adobe Commerce is critical to restore accurate conversion tracking. Concurrently, optimizing Klaviyo\'s email engagement strategy to bolster the interrupted flow at critical touchpoints, particularly the abandoned cart flow, should help mitigate loss. Any lag or error in connecting sessions to orders should be fixed to ensure streamlined and accurate ecommerce operations.','openai/gpt-4o','2026-08-04 02:01:59','2026-08-04 02:01:59','internal',0),(26,42,'1. **GA4 Metric**: Conversion rate fell to 2.52% in the anomaly period, lower than prior at 3.06%.\n2. **Adobe Commerce Orders**: Orders dropped from 134 to 112 between the periods (-16.42%).\n3. **Klaviyo Revenue**: Zero revenue from emails on August 3 vs. significant revenue on July 21. \n4. **GA4 vs Adobe Commerce**: On July 21, GA4 showed 357 sessions and Adobe recorded 2 orders (0.56% conversion). On August 3, GA4 had 201 sessions but Adobe had only 1 order.\n5. **Klaviyo Marketing**: Abandoned cart flow conversions dropped from 8 on July 21 to 0 by August 3.','**What Happened**\nThe purchase funnel conversion rate declined from **3.06%** to **2.52%** in the last 30 days, leading to a **17.5% decrease** in the overall conversion.\n| Metric | Prior Period | Anomaly Period | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Conversion Rate | 3.06% | 2.52% | **-17.5%** |\n| Orders (GA4) | 134 | 112 | -16.42% |\n| Revenue (Adobe) | $26,456 | $21,846 | -17.42% |\n\n**Cross-Platform Evidence**\nThere was a notable decrease in sessions and orders primarily from direct and organic sources during the anomaly period.\n- **July 21**: GA4 recorded **357 sessions** vs. Adobe **2 orders**\n- **August 3**: GA4 recorded **201 sessions** vs. Adobe **1 order**, highlighting a significant drop.\n- **Klaviyo**: Email-attributed revenue fell from **$7,839.60** to **$0** between July 21 and August 3.\n\n**Behavioral Analysis**\nNo Clarity data was provided hence, no direct behavioral analysis was conducted. However, declining conversion rates often align with increased friction or usability issues.\n\n**Root Cause Assessment**\nThe drop in email campaign effectiveness, specifically the “Abandoned Cart Flow,” from high revenue days, combined with almost no conversions from emails toward the anomaly end, suggests that ineffective emails or delivery issues could be significant.\n> Klaviyo data confirms email revenue fell to $0 while Order conversion plummeted.\n> GA4 data highlights reduced effective sessions during the last days of July into August.\n','The identified root cause for the decline in funnel conversion appears to be linked to a significant drop in email performance, specifically the \'Abandoned Cart Flow\' and overall email campaign effectiveness during the anomaly period. This aligns with a sharp decrease in Klaviyo-attributed revenue and correlates with decreased order conversions in Adobe Commerce.\n\nThe impact of this decline includes a reduced total revenue of approximately **$4,610** attributed to the anomaly period\'s decrease in conversion and orders. This suggests that email effectiveness, specifically abandoned cart flow, must be immediately optimized or troubleshot to address and rectify any deliverability, engagement, or conversion blockages.\n\nTo address this, it is essential to thoroughly review and repair email marketing strategies and processes, hereby improving both deliverability and engagement, which directly contribute to regained conversion metrics and revenue alignment. Also, ensure consistent auditing and monitoring of behavioral analytics and user feedback to minimize friction in the purchase funnel.','openai/gpt-4o','2026-08-04 02:02:26','2026-08-04 02:02:26','internal',0),(27,43,'1. **Clarity Quickbacks**: Increased from **42.4** to **53.5** on August 5 — highlights the behavioral anomaly\n2. **GA4 vs Adobe Commerce**: On August 3, **100 sessions** in GA4 with **0 orders** in Adobe Commerce (**0% conversion**), indicating severe drop in engagement\n3. **Clarity Correlation**: Quickbacks were **149** on July 30 with friction score at **15.2**, corresponding to **0 orders** recorded in Adobe Commerce — direct impact on revenue and orders','**What Happened**\nThe quickback rate increased by **26.1%**, from **42.4** to **53.5** quickbacks, as detected by Clarity\'s behavioral signals on August 5. This indicates users are leaving the site more rapidly, suggesting potential issues with content relevance or engagement.\n\n| Metric | Prior Period | Anomaly Period | Change |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Quickbacks | **42.4** | **53.5** | **+26.1%** |\n\n**Cross-Platform Evidence**\nAnalysis of GA4 and Adobe Commerce shows a mismatch between sessions and orders, indicating disengaged users:\n\n- **July 29**: **85 GA4 sessions**, **3 orders** in Adobe Commerce (**3.96% conversion**)\n- **August 3**: **100 GA4 sessions**, **0 orders** in Adobe Commerce\n\n**Behavioral Analysis**\nClarity data highlights increased friction and quickbacks:\n\n- **July 30 and 31**: High quickbacks (**149** and **139**) with **script errors** at **84** each day\n- **Friction score**: Spiked to **57.3** on July 25 with a **398** script error count\n> High friction scores and quickbacks on July 30-31, days with high script errors, correlate with low conversion rates.\n\n**Root Cause Assessment**\nThe most likely root cause is increased script errors leading to high friction scores, worsening user experience and driving quickbacks.\n\n- Script errors on **July 25** and **July 30-31** directly align with the highest number of quickbacks\n- Decreased overall engagement time correlates with increased quickbacks, implying content issues exacerbated by technical failures','The confirmed root cause of the increased quickback rate is a combination of content inefficiency and technical issues, particularly script errors, affecting user navigation and experience. The impact includes a notable decline in conversions on critical days, as evident on August 3 with zero conversions despite a full session load, costing potential revenue gains. To address this, the recommendation is to rectify script errors and align content with user intent and expectations to prevent further drop in engagements and conversions.','openai/gpt-4o','2026-08-05 02:00:34','2026-08-05 02:00:34','internal',0);
